-    <header>
-      <div class="eyebrow">Analysis · AEGIS 0.13.0</div>
-      <h1>How much does the workflow cost, and what does it buy?</h1>
-      <p class="lede">A measurement of AEGIS overhead on three real repositories: the share of an agent's tool calls, tokens and git history that goes to the workflow, set against the defects the workflow demonstrably caught.</p>
-      <div class="meta">
-        <span>Date <b>2026-09-17</b></span>
-        <span>Hosts <b>Claude Code, Codex Desktop</b></span>
-        <span>Evidence <b>78 session transcripts, 3 git histories, 151 task journals, 1,111 requirement files</b></span>
-      </div>
-    </header>
-
-    <section id="summary">
-      <div class="callout">
-        <div class="eyebrow">In one paragraph</div>
-        <p>Across three repositories and two hosts, AEGIS takes about a quarter of an agent's tool calls: 23 to 29 percent in Claude Code, 13 to 26 percent in Codex. The instruction text itself is cheap, around 4 percent of a turn's context. The cost is round trips: every <code>check</code>, <code>summary</code>, journal edit and requirement file re-reads the whole conversation. What the overhead buys varies sharply by repository. The one where the two-verdict review is run as written recorded 49 findings that sent work back to build. The two where it is not recorded one finding and none. Worth it for code that has to live, when the review is actually run. Not worth it for diagnosis, build and packaging work with no test seam, or a fix an existing requirement already covers.</p>
-      </div>
-    </section>
-
-    <section id="method">
-      <h2>Method and signals</h2>
-      <p>Three repositories were measured, all governed by AEGIS 0.13.0 with explicit review enabled and two fix cycles. None is named here.</p>
-      <ul>
-        <li><strong>Repository A</strong> is a Python desktop application with a long history. It migrated from an earlier gate-based workflow in July, so its git churn is windowed to the AEGIS period and the removal of the old workflow's files is left out.</li>
-        <li><strong>Repository B</strong> is an appliance with a Go core and a React interface, under AEGIS for its entire history.</li>
-        <li><strong>Repository C</strong> is a fork of a twenty-year-old C and C++ open-source stack, where the agent's work is mostly cross-builds, packaging and hardware drivers rather than unit-testable features.</li>
-      </ul>
-      <p>Every tool call in every transcript with at least 15 calls was classified by a script into nine buckets from the tool name and its input: AEGIS CLI run, instruction read, record read, record write, product read, product write, test or lint, git, other. Codex sessions were matched by working directory; subagent transcripts were excluded. The classifier is a set of regular expressions, so treat shares as accurate to a few points. Everything below is observed in files. Nothing is inferred from what the agent said it did.</p>
-      <div class="legend">
-        <span><span class="sig ok">green</span>within the range the workflow is designed for</span>
-        <span><span class="sig warn">orange</span>worth attention, usually a cost that can be cut</span>
-        <span><span class="sig bad">red</span>a problem: the workflow is not doing what it claims, or costs more than it returns</span>
-      </div>
-    </section>
-
-    <section id="repos">
-      <h2>Three repositories compared</h2>
-
-      <div class="tablewrap">
-        <table>
-          <caption>Profile</caption>
