-            <tr><td>AEGIS share of tool calls, Codex</td><td class="n"><span class="sig warn">26.4%</span> · 15 sessions</td><td class="n"><span class="sig warn">22.8%</span> · 3 sessions</td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">13.4%</span> · 3 sessions</td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Output tokens, all sessions</td><td class="n">9.0 M</td><td class="n">21.9 M</td><td class="n">2.7 M</td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Journal length, median · max lines</td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">102 · 434</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig warn">277 · 948</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">134 · 333</span></td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Commits touching only workflow files</td><td class="n"><span class="sig warn">79 · 25%</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">18 · 12%</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">3 · 3%</span></td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Fingerprint JSON on disk</td><td class="n"><span class="sig warn">2.4 MB</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig bad">6.2 MB</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig bad">3.2 MB</span></td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Fingerprint churn vs product churn</td><td class="n"><span class="sig warn">30%</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig bad">78%</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">14%</span></td></tr>
-            <tr><td><code>check</code> today: errors · warnings · notes</td><td class="n"><span class="sig warn">0 · 11 · 1</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig warn">0 · 6 · 3</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig bad">0 · 15 · 7</span></td></tr>
-          </tbody>
-        </table>
-      </div>
-      <p>Repository A's warnings include two <code>secret-in-diff</code> hits and two <code>review-state</code> findings. Repository C's are thirteen <code>stale-paths</code>, requirement files that point at source that no longer exists where they say. Neither is fatal, and both are the drift that accumulates when close-out happens outside the workflow.</p>
-
-      <div class="tablewrap">
-        <table>
-          <caption>What the workflow recorded catching</caption>
-          <thead><tr><th></th><th class="n">Repository A</th><th class="n">Repository B</th><th class="n">Repository C</th></tr></thead>
-          <tbody>
-            <tr><td>Red-then-green lines with command and exit code</td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">89</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">309</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">21</span></td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Red lines per journal</td><td class="n"><span class="sig warn">0.9</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">6.9</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">2.1</span></td></tr>
-            <tr><td><code>changes_required</code> review verdicts</td><td class="n"><span class="sig bad">1</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">49</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig bad">0</span></td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Returns from validate to an earlier stage</td><td class="n"><span class="sig warn">3</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">27</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig bad">0</span></td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Review rounds beyond the first</td><td class="n"><span class="sig bad">3</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">61</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig bad">0</span></td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Deviation notices · of which tool noise</td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">65 · 2</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig warn">159 · 20</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig ok">18 · 3</span></td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Tasks blocked by review</td><td class="n">0</td><td class="n">2</td><td class="n">0</td></tr>
-          </tbody>
-        </table>
-      </div>
-
-      <div class="flag">
-        <div class="eyebrow">Red · the row that matters</div>
-        <p>All three repositories have explicit review enabled with the same two fix cycles. Repository B recorded 49 <code>changes_required</code> verdicts across 45 journals. Repository A recorded one across 96, Repository C none across 10. A review that approves 95 of 96 candidates on the first pass is not reviewing. The session study below shows the mechanism: the agent records both verdicts as approved without running two separate passes, and the advisory <code>check</code> has no rule that notices. In Repository A the overhead of the review stage was paid and its benefit was not collected.</p>
-      </div>
-      <div class="watch">
-        <div class="eyebrow">Orange · Repository A is a fix factory</div>
-        <p>Ninety-three journals opened in August alone, 59 of them fixes, and only 15 took the quick path. A fix per journal is where a 25 percent bookkeeping-only commit rate comes from: each small change pays a frame, a build red line, a validate pass and a close-out commit. That cadence would suit the quick path more often, or one journal per batch of related fixes.</p>
-      </div>
-      <div class="watch">
-        <div class="eyebrow">Orange · Repository C is the wrong shape for test-first</div>
-        <p>Cross-builds, packaging and a USB protocol have no unit-test seam, so nine of 27 requirements are verified by hand and 13 requirement files already point at stale paths. AEGIS still produced a usable audit trail, which the repository's own readme names as the point, but the build stage's red-then-green rule became a manual observation a third of the time. The lowest Codex share, 13 percent, is consistent with that: fewer stage transitions because there was less to prove.</p>
-      </div>
-    </section>
-
-    <section id="share">
-      <h2>Share per session, one dot each</h2>
-      <p>Aggregates hide the shape. Every dot below is one session's AEGIS share of tool calls; the large dark dot is the median. Sessions with fewer than 15 tool calls are excluded.</p>
-      <div class="chart" aria-label="Per-session AEGIS share">
-        <div class="group eyebrow">Repository A</div>
-        <div class="strip-row"><span class="label">Claude Code · 24</span><span class="strip"><i style="left:0%"></i><i style="left:0%"></i><i style="left:0%"></i><i style="left:0%"></i><i style="left:0%"></i><i style="left:1%"></i><i style="left:2%"></i><i style="left:4%"></i><i style="left:4%"></i><i style="left:5%"></i><i style="left:7%"></i><i style="left:9%"></i><i style="left:14%"></i><i style="left:20%"></i><i style="left:24%"></i><i style="left:29%"></i><i style="left:29%"></i><i style="left:37%"></i><i style="left:42%"></i><i style="left:43%"></i><i style="left:44%"></i><i style="left:45%"></i><i style="left:46%"></i><i style="left:47%"></i><i class="med" style="left:17%"></i></span><span class="val">md 17%</span></div>
-        <div class="strip-row"><span class="label">Codex · 15</span><span class="strip"><i style="left:0%"></i><i style="left:5%"></i><i style="left:5%"></i><i style="left:6%"></i><i style="left:8%"></i><i style="left:16%"></i><i style="left:19%"></i><i style="left:21%"></i><i style="left:22%"></i><i style="left:26%"></i><i style="left:34%"></i><i style="left:34%"></i><i style="left:35%"></i><i style="left:40%"></i><i style="left:46%"></i><i class="med" style="left:21%"></i></span><span class="val">md 21%</span></div>
-        <div class="group eyebrow">Repository B</div>
-        <div class="strip-row"><span class="label">Claude Code · 26</span><span class="strip"><i style="left:0%"></i><i style="left:0%"></i><i style="left:3%"></i><i style="left:6%"></i><i style="left:6%"></i><i style="left:12%"></i><i style="left:14%"></i><i style="left:17%"></i><i style="left:19%"></i><i style="left:20%"></i><i style="left:22%"></i><i style="left:25%"></i><i style="left:26%"></i><i style="left:27%"></i><i style="left:28%"></i><i style="left:29%"></i><i style="left:32%"></i><i style="left:32%"></i><i style="left:38%"></i><i style="left:38%"></i><i style="left:41%"></i><i style="left:41%"></i><i style="left:42%"></i><i style="left:45%"></i><i style="left:45%"></i><i style="left:53%"></i><i class="med" style="left:26.5%"></i></span><span class="val">md 27%</span></div>
-        <div class="strip-row"><span class="label">Codex · 3</span><span class="strip"><i style="left:21%"></i><i style="left:23%"></i><i style="left:28%"></i><i class="med" style="left:23%"></i></span><span class="val">md 23%</span></div>
-        <div class="group eyebrow">Repository C</div>
-        <div class="strip-row"><span class="label">Claude Code · 7</span><span class="strip"><i style="left:1%"></i><i style="left:6%"></i><i style="left:19%"></i><i style="left:23%"></i><i style="left:26%"></i><i style="left:33%"></i><i style="left:42%"></i><i class="med" style="left:23%"></i></span><span class="val">md 23%</span></div>
-        <div class="strip-row"><span class="label">Codex · 3</span><span class="strip"><i style="left:4%"></i><i style="left:5%"></i><i style="left:19%"></i><i class="med" style="left:5%"></i></span><span class="val">md 5%</span></div>
-        <div class="axis"><span></span><span class="ticks"><span>0%</span><span>20%</span><span>40%</span><span>60%</span><span>80%</span><span>100%</span></span><span></span></div>
-        <p class="chart-note">The distribution is bimodal. Sessions at 0 to 7 percent are diagnosis, questions and repository setup. Sessions at 40 percent and above are stage-transition heavy: many small tasks in one session, or checkpoint mode with a stop after every stage. The 20 to 30 percent middle is a single full-path task run in autopilot.</p>
-      </div>
-
-      <div class="callout">
-        <div class="eyebrow">The two hosts spend the overhead differently</div>
-        <p>Claude Code runs the CLI and writes records: in Repository A, 7.8 percent of its calls are <code>aegis.py</code> runs and 11.3 percent are journal or requirement writes. Codex on the same repository runs the CLI in 1 percent of calls and instead reads records in 21 percent of them. Same share, opposite mechanism. A rule added to <code>check</code> reaches the first host on every transition and the second only when a human runs it.</p>
-      </div>
-    </section>
-
-    <section id="session">
-      <h2>One session, both ways</h2>
-      <p>One Repository A session lasted just under two hours and ran the same problem both ways. The first six user turns asked why a hosted model provider returned a differently shaped answer than local models for a text-splitting step, and how to skip a refused chunk while preserving resume. The operator's words were "do not use aegis, just answer". The seventh turn chose an option and said "implement this on master branch, using aegis. Do not commit."</p>
-
-      <div class="split">
-        <div>
-          <div class="eyebrow">Turns 1 to 6</div>
-          <div class="phase">Diagnosis, workflow off</div>
-          <dl>
-            <dt>Wall clock</dt><dd>61 min</dd>
-            <dt>Assistant messages</dt><dd>155</dd>
-            <dt>Tool calls</dt><dd>86</dd>
-            <dt>AEGIS-related calls</dt><dd>0</dd>
-            <dt>Output tokens</dt><dd>168 k</dd>
-            <dt>Cache-read tokens</dt><dd>22 M</dd>
-          </dl>
-        </div>
-        <div>
-          <div class="eyebrow">Turns 7 to 9</div>
-          <div class="phase">Implementation, workflow on</div>
-          <dl>
-            <dt>Wall clock</dt><dd>48 min</dd>
-            <dt>Assistant messages</dt><dd>229</dd>
-            <dt>Tool calls</dt><dd>130</dd>
-            <dt>AEGIS-related calls</dt><dd class="hi">44 · 34%</dd>
-            <dt>Output tokens</dt><dd>254 k</dd>
-            <dt>Cache-read tokens</dt><dd>77 M</dd>
-          </dl>
-        </div>
-      </div>
-
-      <p>The 44 workflow calls split into 19 CLI runs, 16 reads and writes of journal and requirement files, and 9 reads of the contract and step files. The stage went <code>frame → specify → design → build → validate → deliver</code> on the full path in autopilot, and produced one journal of 434 lines, four requirement files, one reconciled knowledge page and a changelog entry.</p>
-
-      <h3>What it caught</h3>
-      <div class="catch">
-        <div class="eyebrow">Green · build, red then green</div>
-        <p>The first green run exposed a completion defect the design note had not named. The journal records it in one line, with the command and the exit code.</p>
-      </div>
-      <div class="catch">
-        <div class="eyebrow">Green · validate, diff review</div>
-        <p>A second call path in the chunk-regeneration function was found to bypass the new guard. Fixed in stage.</p>
-      </div>
-      <div class="catch">
-        <div class="eyebrow">Green · validate, two-verdict review on the rerun</div>
-        <p>The specification-compliance pass found that a second, unguarded provider call on the ambience path would still end the run on the operator's own project settings. The implementation-quality pass found skipped chunk ids seeded from a snapshot the resume logic had already discarded. Both were fixed and regression-tested, red without the guard and green with it.</p>
-      </div>
-
-      <h3>What it did not do on its own</h3>
-      <div class="flag">
-        <div class="eyebrow">Red · the review was stamped, not run</div>
-        <p>The round-one verdicts were first recorded as approved without the two passes being run separately. The operator asked "did you run the 2 separate validators?", the agent withdrew the verdicts, reran both passes and only then found the ambience defect. This is the same mechanism the cross-repository table shows at scale: one <code>changes_required</code> in 96 journals.</p>
-      </div>
-      <p>The strict full check was skipped as well, and said so:</p>
-      <pre><code>DEVIATION build: ran the targeted modules instead of the 63-file impacted set — the user limited verification to targeted tests and will exercise the app by hand</code></pre>
-      <p>This is the advisory design working exactly as documented. Nothing blocked the shortcut, the journal made it visible, and a human had to insist.</p>
-
-      <div class="callout">
-        <div class="eyebrow">Why two verdicts and not one</div>
-        <p>The implementation-quality pass inspects the code the agent wrote, and by that standard the ambience path was invisible because the agent had not touched it. The specification-compliance pass reads the requirement, here "continue through the last chunk", and asks what the whole loop must do. A single merged review inherits the design's framing of where the seam is, which is precisely how the defect survived the first round.</p>
-      </div>
-    </section>
-
-    <section id="caught">
-      <h2>What the overhead bought, where it was collected</h2>
-      <p>Repository B is the one where the workflow was run as written, so it is the honest measure of the upside. Counted from its 45 journals, not from prose:</p>
-      <ul>
-        <li><strong>309 red-then-green lines</strong> with command and exit code. The test-first rule was followed, not narrated.</li>
-        <li><strong>49 <code>changes_required</code> verdicts and 27 returns to an earlier stage.</strong> Findings were fixed under proof rather than patched in place.</li>
-        <li><strong>One task ran five review rounds and ended blocked</strong>, with concrete findings each round: a rate cap instantiated per interface so the documented global bound multiplied, a label test that decoded a second in-memory object instead of the emitted artifact, an audit-outbox retry with no automated oracle. A green suite does not reveal those.</li>
-        <li><strong>159 deviation notices, 20 of them tool noise.</strong> The rest name narrowed verification, skipped checks and accepted warnings, which is the point of the notice.</li>
-      </ul>
-      <p>Repository A collected the build-stage benefit, 89 red lines and a low noise rate, and almost none of the review-stage benefit. Repository C collected an audit trail and little else that a test could prove.</p>
-    </section>
-
-    <section id="cost">
-      <h2>Where the cost lives</h2>
-      <p>It is tempting to blame the instruction text. The numbers say otherwise.</p>
-      <div class="tablewrap">
-        <table>
-          <caption>What a full-path task reads before it starts</caption>
-          <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th class="n">Words</th></tr></thead>
-          <tbody>
-            <tr><td>Workflow contract (<code>.agentic/AGENTS.md</code>)</td><td class="n">3,762</td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Repository contract and host note</td><td class="n">2,071</td></tr>
-            <tr><td>One stage file, typical</td><td class="n">1,100</td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Task and requirement templates</td><td class="n">1,274</td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Project and environment context</td><td class="n">2,996</td></tr>
-            <tr class="total"><td>Total, roughly 15,000 tokens</td><td class="n">11,203</td></tr>
-          </tbody>
-        </table>
-      </div>
-      <p>That is read once and cached. The implementation phase of the session above averaged about 336,000 tokens of context per assistant message, so the workflow text was around 4 percent of each turn. The other 96 percent is the conversation itself, and every extra round trip re-reads all of it. Forty-four bookkeeping round trips are where the 77 million cache-read tokens came from.</p>
-
-      <div class="tablewrap">
-        <table>
-          <caption>Repository B: lines added plus deleted over the whole history</caption>
-          <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th class="n">Lines</th><th class="n">Relative to product source</th></tr></thead>
-          <tbody>
-            <tr><td>Product source</td><td class="n">100,122</td><td class="n">100%</td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Product tests</td><td class="n">70,936</td><td class="n">71%</td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Requirements and specs</td><td class="n">29,745</td><td class="n">30%</td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Task journals</td><td class="n">16,286</td><td class="n">16%</td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Knowledge and context</td><td class="n">3,519</td><td class="n">4%</td></tr>
-            <tr><td>Anchor and evolution fingerprint JSON</td><td class="n"><span class="sig bad">133,441</span></td><td class="n"><span class="sig bad">133%</span></td></tr>
-          </tbody>
-        </table>
-      </div>
-      <p>Journals, requirements and knowledge together are half the product-source churn in Repository B, which agrees with the tool-call share once tests are counted. The fingerprint JSON is a different matter. It exists only to feed the <code>evolution</code> report, it is the largest single line of churn in Repository B's history, 48,000 lines in Repository A's AEGIS period and 69,000 in Repository C's, and no reader ever opens it. It is the one pure-noise item in all three repositories.</p>
-      <p>Two secondary costs are visible in the records. Repository B's journal logs have a median of 28 entries and a maximum of 227, which is why the current rules consolidate inner-loop retries. And every repository's advisory <code>check</code> carries warnings today, so the bookkeeping drifts between tasks:</p>
-      <pre><code>Repository A   summary: errors=0 warnings=11 notes=1   secret-in-diff, review-state, unticked-deliver
-Repository B   summary: errors=0 warnings=6  notes=3   stale-paths, unticked-deliver
-Repository C   summary: errors=0 warnings=15 notes=7   stale-paths, scope-drift, journal-header</code></pre>
-    </section>
-
-    <section id="verdict">
-      <h2>Verdict</h2>
-      <div class="verdict go">
-        <h3>Use the workflow when the code has to live, and run the review</h3>
-        <ul>
-          <li>A feature on a codebase you will still be changing in six months.</li>
-          <li>Anything with a data format, a permission rule, a resume contract or an error path a user will hit.</li>
-          <li>Any session where nobody will personally read the whole diff. The requirement text is what gave the review a reason to look outside the code the agent wrote.</li>
-          <li>Only when the two-verdict review is actually run as two passes. Repository A paid for the stage and collected one finding in 96 tasks.</li>
-        </ul>
-      </div>
-      <div class="verdict stop">
-        <h3>Leave the agent alone when</h3>
-        <ul>
-          <li>The ask is diagnosis or explanation. The first hour of the session above was correctly run without the workflow.</li>
-          <li>The change is one file and an existing requirement already covers the behavior. That is what the quick path is for, and Repository A used it for only 15 of 59 fixes.</li>
-          <li>The work is a cross-build, a package or a driver with no test seam. Keep the journal for the audit trail if the record matters, but do not expect test-first to mean anything there.</li>
-          <li>The code is exploratory or throwaway and will not be validated or delivered.</li>
-        </ul>
-      </div>
-    </section>
-
-    <section id="changes">
-      <h2>Four changes that cut the cost or collect the benefit</h2>
-      <ol>
-        <li><strong>Make the two-verdict review non-skippable in practice.</strong> A strict <code>check</code> finding when a validate stage report carries approved verdicts with no per-verdict log line. This is the only change that turns Repository A's red row green, and it costs nothing in tool calls.</li>
-        <li><strong>Stop committing anchor and evolution JSON.</strong> Ignore the files, or have <code>evolution</code> write a short added, modified and removed summary into the journal. This removes the largest line of churn in all three histories.</li>
-        <li><strong>Autopilot by default for trusted tasks.</strong> Checkpoint stops are what push a session from the 25 percent middle to the 45 percent tail, and the pre-publication approval remains in both modes.</li>
-        <li><strong>Batch small fixes under one journal, or take the quick path more often.</strong> One journal per fix is what gives Repository A a 25 percent bookkeeping-only commit rate. The quick path was designed for exactly that cadence and was used a quarter of the time.</li>
-      </ol>
-    </section>
-
-    <footer>
-      Figures were measured on 2026-09-17 from local session transcripts, git history and <code>.agentic</code> records of three repositories. Tool-call classification is heuristic and accurate to a few points. Repository A's git figures cover its AEGIS period only, from the migration on 2026-07-18. Sample output quoted here is evidence of what was recorded, not of a fresh test run.
-    </footer>
-  </main>
-</div>
-</body>
